n\\overline{T}_P \n%= \\overline{\\big\\{ 4,6,8,9,10 \\big\\}} \n= \\big\\{1,3,5,7,9 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_Q = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,6 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \nand its complement is\n\n$$\n\\overline{T}_Q \n%= \\overline{\\big\\{ \\big\\}} \n= \\big\\{ 5,7,8,9,10  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & ={2,4,6,8,10}\\cap {1,2,3,4,6} \\\\  & ={2,4,6} \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & ={1,3,5,7,9}\\cap {5,7,8,9,10} \\\\  & ={5,7,9} \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\  & ={2,4,6}\\cup {5,7,9} \\\\  & ={2,4,5,6,7,9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534669 17592186045417 false] [17592186198107 1071 "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\qquad$ $A(x):$ $x<4.$ $\\qquad$ $B(x):$ $\\dfrac x2< 2.$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x) \\Leftrightarrow B(x)?$\n\n- $x<4$ if and only if $\\dfrac x2< 2.$\n\n- $x<4$ is equivalent to $\\dfrac x2< 2.$\n\n- If $x<4$ then $\\dfrac x2< 2,$ or if $\\dfrac x2< 2$ then $x <4.$" 13194139534669 17592186045417 false] [17592186198107 1071 "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\qquad$ $A(x):$ $x<4.$ $\\qquad$ $B(x):$ $\\dfrac x2< 2.$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x) \\Leftrightarrow B(x)?$\n\nI. $x<4$ if and only if $\\dfrac x2< 2.$\n\nII. $x<4$ is equivalent to $\\dfrac x2< 2.$\n\nIII. If $x<4$ then $\\dfrac x2< 2,$ or if $\\dfrac x2< 2$ then $x <4.$" 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186198260 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431925 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186198261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431924 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186198261 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431926 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186198516 1050 720.0 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186199525 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **implication** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Rightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis implication is false whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is false for a specific set of respective variable assignments $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nNow, recall the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$\nP\\Rightarrow Q \\equiv \\lnot P \\lor Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following universal set:\n\n$$\nU = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's define the following predicates over $U{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime\n\n- $Q(x):$ $9$ is divisible by $x$\n\nTo find the truth set of $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x),$ we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = \\left\\{ 2,3,5,7 \\right\\}.$ As a result, the complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=\\overset{―}{\\{2,3,5,7\\}}=\\{1,4,6,8,9\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_Q = \\big\\{ 1,3,9 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =\\{1,4,6,8,9\\}\\cup \\{1,3,9\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,3,4,6,8,9\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186199525 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **implication** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Rightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$$\n\nThis implication is false whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is false for a specific set of respective variable assignments $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nNow, recall the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$P\\Rightarrow Q \\equiv \\lnot P \\lor Q$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P\\Rightarrow Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\ & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following universal set:\n\n$$U = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 \\big\\}$$\n\nLet's define the following predicates over $U{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime\n- $Q(x):$ $9$ is divisible by $x$\n\nTo find the truth set of $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x),$ we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = \\left\\{ 2,3,5,7 \\right\\}.$ As a result, the complement is $$\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{2,3,5,7}}={1,4,6,8,9}.\\end{aligned}$$\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $$T_Q = \\big\\{ 1,3,9 \\big\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P\\Rightarrow Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\ & ={1,4,6,8,9}\\cup {1,3,9} \\\\ & ={1,3,4,6,8,9}.\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139534669 17592186045417 false] [17592186199526 1064 "Since implication and equivalence of predicates are simply generalizations of logical operations on statements, all the properties of these operations can be translated to the corresponding operations on predicates.\n\nIn particular, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P⇒Q & ≡¬P∨Q \\\\ P⇔Q & ≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨P) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's remind ourselves of the grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals that we've encountered so far:\n\nFor conditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Rightarrow Q$\n\n- *If $P,$ then $Q$*\n\n- *$P$ implies $Q$*\n\nFor biconditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$\n\n- *$P$ is equivalent to $Q$*\n\n- *$P$ if and only if $Q$*\n\n- *If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$*" 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186199526 1064 "Since implication and equivalence of predicates are simply generalizations of logical operations on statements, all the properties of these operations can be translated to the corresponding operations on predicates.\n\nIn particular, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}P\\Rightarrow Q & ≡¬P∨Q \\\\ P⇔Q & ≡(P\\Rightarrow Q)∧(Q\\Rightarrow P) \\\\ & ≡(¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨P) \\\\ & ≡(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, let's remind ourselves of the grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals that we've encountered so far:\n\nFor conditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Rightarrow Q$\n- *If $P,$ then $Q$*\n- *$P$ implies $Q$*\n\nFor biconditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$\n- *$P$ is equivalent to $Q$*\n- *$P$ if and only if $Q$*\n- *If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$*" 13194139534669 17592186045417 false] [17592186199527 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Again, we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **equivalence** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis predicate is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values for a specific set of variable assignments $x_1,x_2,\\ldots, x_n.$\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)\n$$\n\nLet's look at some examples. Assume $x\\in\\mathbb R$ throughout.\n\n- The following statement is an equivalence (i.e., it is true).\n\n$$\nx + 2 = 5 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=3\n$$\n\nThis statement is true because \n\n$$\n\\left(x+2=5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=3\\right)\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\left(x=3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x+2 = 5\\right).\n$$\n\n- The following statement is **not** an equivalence (i.e., it is false).\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=2\n$$\n\nThis statement is false because \n\n$$\n\\left(x=2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  x^2 = 4\\right) \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\left(x^2=4\\quad \\not\\Rightarrow\\quad x=2\\right).\n$$\n\nHowever, the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 2\n$$" 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186199527 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Again, we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **equivalence** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows: $$P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$$\n\nThis predicate is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values for a specific set of variable assignments $x_1,x_2,\\ldots, x_n.$\n\n$$P \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)$$\n\nLet's look at some examples. Assume $x\\in\\mathbb R$ throughout.\n\n- The following statement is an equivalence (i.e., it is true). $$x + 2 = 5 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=3$$ This statement is true because $$\\left(x+2=5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=3\\right)\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\left(x=3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x+2 = 5\\right).$$\n- The following statement is **not** an equivalence (i.e., it is false). $$x^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=2$$ This statement is false because $$\\left(x=2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x^2 = 4\\right) \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\left(x^2=4\\quad \\not\\Rightarrow\\quad x=2\\right).$$ However, the following statement is true: $$x^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 2$$" 13194139534669 17592186045417 false]